+# Architecture — EPIC-Queue-v2
+
+> Master reference. Reads top-to-bottom for a complete picture of the v2 dispatch system: components, data flow, state machine, contracts, risks, tradeoffs. Cross-links to per-story specs in `implementation-plan.md`, the operational runbook in `overwatch-runbook.md`, and the diagnosis in `diagnosis.md`.
+
+## 1. System Overview
+
+The dispatch fleet is a closed-loop system where developer agents (Dan, Daisy, Devon, Derrick) claim stories, execute SDLC phases, and submit work for review. Manager-role agents (Morris) and the human operator (Mark) form an "overwatch layer" that handles redispatch, knowledge curation, and escalations the agents can't resolve themselves.
+
+v2 reorganizes this around **three primitives the v1 system lacks**:
+1. **Lease** (a token-bound, time-bound claim, separate from the row).
+2. **Event log** (the only source of truth for state; the row is a projection cache).
+3. **Versioned contract** (poller and route refuse to interoperate across incompatible versions).
+
+It then layers two **cognitive primitives** the system has never had:
+4. **Knowledge layer** (Q7) — `tech-gc-knowledgebase` becomes shared memory across runs. Read at story-start, written at story-complete, dedupes questions.
+5. **Apprenticeship loop** (Q9) — every Mark decision is logged; patterns become rules; Mark's required involvement declines quarterly.
+
+And one **safety primitive**:
+6. **Self-healing** (Q8) — question budget, stuck-agent heuristics, needs_info TTL. Stuck agents get released to attention instead of burning tokens.
+
