+# Diagnosis — Why the Queue Keeps Breaking
+
+> Independent diagnosis written before merging with the rescue-agent's plan. Anchored in the 2-week incident record (action logs 2026-04-16 → 2026-04-30) and a code reading of `tech_dev_agents/ops_console/routes/dispatch.py` and `deployment/hermes/dispatch_poller.py`.
+
+## Symptoms (from the record)
+
+| Date | Story / commit | Symptom |
+|------|----------------|---------|
+| 2026-04-19 | STORY-459/461/462 dispatch | 409 already-active loops on rework |
+| 2026-04-22 | Morris fleet assessment | ~34% queue success rate, 50 dispatches |
+| 2026-04-23 | STORY-552 | Ghost-claim race: Devon SDK exited without `/complete`, next poll claimed STORY-551 while STORY-549 stayed orphaned |
+| 2026-04-24 | STORY-574 / 798 | `/release` refused `in_progress`, only `claimed`; needs_info_set without committed QUESTION.md drove 409 ghost-claim loop |
+| 2026-04-26 to 04-27 | STORY-740 (PRs #170, #171, #173) | 4 Pydantic surfaces + SQL CHECK + partial index drifted from each other; 36 hours, 4 reactive PRs |
+| 2026-04-28 | PR #218 review (STORY-741) | `_classify_failure` was dead code in production — `error_message` never passed from real call sites |
+| 2026-04-29 | STORY-007–017 (11 stories) | All 11 reached `failed`, all 11 had `failure_reason=NULL`, downstream self-healing had no signal |
+| 2026-04-30 | STORY-795 | Head-of-line outage: STORY-633 sat at queue head with ambiguous dep, every poll returned 204 even though 5 stories were eligible behind it |
+| 2026-05-01 | `b8991bb`, `6307d6b` | Two more reactive dispatch fixes in 24h: rework_of visibility, attention queue routing |
+| Today | This session | Repo-qualified claim, bootstrap re-entrant migration, `try/except` on `next_pending` signature |
+
+## Root cause
+
+**The queue is a row-store with mutating status + scattered policy. There is no canonical event log, no lease primitive, and no producer/consumer contract version.**
+
+Five concrete consequences. Each is visible in the code.
+
+### D1 — State lives in a single mutable row, not in a log
+
+`dispatch_items` is read AND written by every component (route, poller, db_service, manual SQL). Columns added in the last 90 days: `rework_of`, `paused_at`, `current_phase`, `phase_started_at`, `needs_info_path`, `target_role`, `claimed_by_role`, `claim_heartbeat_at`, `failure_reason`, `prior_status`, `cross_story_reference`, `attention_path` (de facto via path-prefix detection).
+
+`dispatch_events` was added in STORY-700 — but it's a *secondary* `emit_event` call in the handler, AFTER the row mutation. If the row write commits and the event emit fails, you have history-state divergence with no replay. If the event emit commits but the row write fails, same. There is no transactional guarantee. There is no derivation. There is no "what was the state at T".
+
+**Code evidence:**
+- `dispatch.py:694` — `await emit_event(story_id, row["repo"], "claimed", agent=...)` is fire-and-forget after `db_svc.claim()` returns.
+- Same pattern at lines 848 (released), 1013 (cancelled), 1291 (completed), 1379 (failed), 1656 (needs_info_set), 1699 (resumed).
+
+### D2 — There is no lease, only a status flag
+
+A claim is `status='claimed' AND claimed_by=X AND claim_heartbeat_at > now() - 5min`. There is no separate lease object with a TTL, no lease token returned to the worker, no "this claim is mine specifically" handle.
+
+So every recovery path has to re-derive ownership from row state:
+- `has_active_claim(agent_name)` (STORY-552)
+- Phantom-claim guard via duration heuristic <30s (`dispatch_poller.py:1000`)
+- Stale-claim release via cron sweep
+- Force-release endpoint requiring 10-char reason for audit (`dispatch.py:759`)
+- Heartbeat watchdog daemon thread in phase runner (STORY-702, STORY-763)
+
+Each of these is correct. None of them exist if the lease is a first-class object with `(job_id, lease_token, expires_at)` in a separate table. The watchdog becomes "delete leases where expires_at < now()", and any subsequent transition with the dead token gets a structured 409.
+
+### D3 — Selection and locking are decoupled across two HTTP calls
+
+`/dispatch/next` selects (filter, role-gate, dep-gate, exclude-iter, returns the row).
+`/dispatch/claim/{story_id}` locks (atomic UPDATE conditional on status='pending').
+
+Race window between them. Even with the `has_active_claim` guard added in STORY-552, the *retry* path (`dispatch_poller.py:1158`) calls `/claim` directly and bypasses `/next` entirely — so the dep-gate, role-gate, and skip-iteration logic from STORY-769/795 had to be duplicated in `/claim` too. They aren't fully duplicated today (`dispatch.py:624-690` only has the role guard and has_active_claim; the dep-gate and skip iteration only live in `/next`).
+
+This is structural, not a bug. As long as selection and locking are two endpoints, every gate has to live in both.
+
+### D4 — Policy is scattered
+
+| Policy concern | Lives in |
+|----------------|----------|
+| Dependency parsing (`DO NOT START until STORY-X`) | route `/next` (line 527) AND poller (`_extract_dependencies` import with try/except fallback) |
+| Role gating (manager vs developer) | route `/next` (lines 515-523) AND route `/claim` (lines 624-654) |
+| Rate-limit detection | poller (stale-log false-positive, fixed in STORY-741) |
+| Failure classification | poller (`_classify_failure` from STORY-701, dead in prod per PR #218 review) |
+| Phantom-claim guard (<30s exit) | poller (line 1000) |
+| Stale-claim auto-release | cron sweep |
+| `needs_info` vs ops attention re-bucket | route's `/dispatch/queue` runtime path-prefix detection (lines 380-400) |
+| Skip-iteration on dep-blocked head | route `/next` only (lines 502-558), MAX_SKIP=32 |
+
+There is no central state machine that says "given event E in state S, transition to S' and apply policy P." STORY-740's `dispatch_state.py` validates the *type signature* of states but not the transition rules — see PR #216 adversarial review M-2: "TRANSITIONS matrix already wrong at day 1; `force_claim()` and `resume_after_answer()` do transitions not listed in TRANSITIONS."
+
+### D5 — No producer/consumer protocol contract
+
+- `dispatch.py:508` — `try: await db_svc.next_pending(exclude_ids=excluded) except TypeError: await db_svc.next_pending(...)`. Runtime fallback for older `db_service` signatures.
+- The rescue-agent's recent fix added `try: from .._extract_dependencies import _extract_dependencies` fallback in dispatch route.
+- Migration 008 was edited in-place (PR #216 H-1 BLOCKING): `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS` is a no-op on already-deployed DBs, requires migration 009.
+- Bootstrap migration 001 made re-entrant after the fact (`deploy.sh` skips if `dispatch_items` exists).
+- Push-code.sh smoke test added in STORY-741 only AFTER a full day was burned on `-w` flag breakage with no post-deploy test.
+
+This is not 5 bugs. This is the absence of a versioned contract between poller, route, db_service, and migrations.
+
+## Why the recent fixes haven't worked
+
+Look at the cadence: STORY-552 (ghost-claim) → STORY-702 (heartbeat) → STORY-723/725 (post-Phase-8 gate, queue test gaps) → STORY-740 (state-machine contract test) → STORY-741 (5 fleet reliability gaps) → STORY-762 (failure_reason persistence) → STORY-763 (zombie heartbeat watchdog) → STORY-769 (dep-aware claim) → STORY-795 (skip-to-next instead of head-of-line 204) → STORY-798/799/800/801 (needs_info loop hardening) → today's repo-qualified claim + bootstrap migration skip + dispatch route compat shims.
+
+These are all *defensive layers* on an architecture that doesn't have the right primitives. Each fix prevents one symptom and creates a new edge-case for the next fix. The list will continue at the same cadence indefinitely until the primitives change.
+
+## What changing the primitives means
+
+Three structural moves cover all five consequences:
+
+1. **Event log is the source of truth.** Row state is a *projection* of the event log, computed by trigger on event INSERT. Every transition writes one event in one transaction. Audit, replay, and debugging all read the log. Closes D1.
+2. **Lease is a first-class object.** `dispatch_leases` table with `(job_id, lease_token, agent, expires_at, heartbeat_at)`. All transitions take `(job_id, lease_token)`. Stale tokens get structured 409. Watchdog deletes expired leases. Closes D2.
+3. **Single atomic `claim-next` endpoint.** `SELECT … FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` + INSERT lease + INSERT event in one transaction. Returns lease token. Replaces `/next` and `/claim/{id}`. Eligibility predicate (deps, role, quarantine) is one server-side function — closes D3 and consolidates D4.
+
+D5 (contract version) is a separate small story: poller sends `X-Worker-Version` header; route enforces minimum on every request; mismatched workers get structured 426 and refuse to claim.
+
+That's the epic. The implementation plan in `implementation-plan.md` decomposes it into 5 stories + 1 deploy-hardening story.
