+# Handoff — for the next reviewer
+
+> Read this first. Then read the documents in the order listed. Then make your pass.
+
+## What this epic is
+
+**EPIC-Queue-v2** — rebuild the dispatch queue so the fleet can self-run reliably. v1 has produced ~20 reactive stories in 2 weeks (STORY-507/531/538/552/700/701/702/723/725/740/741/762/763/769/795/798–802) and is still producing fixes daily. The pattern is the bug, not any individual fix.
+
+## The verdict so far (rounds 1 + 2)
+
+After two passes (rescue agent + critical review), the plan covers:
+
+- **Plumbing fix (Q1-Q6):** lease-based atomic claim, event-sourced state, versioned contract, immutable deploy artifact, single-window cutover. This addresses the *technical* defect (D1-D5 in `diagnosis.md`).
+- **Cognition layer (Q7-Q8):** gc-knowledgebase as cross-run memory, question-budget enforcement, stuck-agent self-healing. This addresses why the fleet's *throughput* sits at ~34% even when the queue itself is "working."
+- **Apprenticeship loop (Q9):** Mark's required involvement declines quarterly by design. Every decision logged, patterns proposed, auto-rules approved, override-guarded. This addresses "truly agentic" — not just running, but progressively self-running.
+- **Operating model:** Mark + Morris cadences in `overwatch-runbook.md`, with the explicit ratchet that Mark's touch rate must decline.
+- **Decision gate:** the entire epic is contingent on a 7-day dispatch-surface freeze failing to stabilize the SLO. If freeze passes, Q1-Q6 cancel; Q7-Q9 ship standalone because they're independently valuable.
+
+## Files in this folder (read in order)
+
+1. **`seed.md`** — epic intro, success criteria SC-1 to SC-14, decomposition (9 stories), document map.
+2. **`diagnosis.md`** — root-cause analysis of v1 (5 structural defects D1-D5 with file:line evidence).
+3. **`architecture.md`** — **master reference.** Components, data flow end-to-end, state machine table, 5 contracts, 10 risks with mitigations + tradeoffs, what this does NOT solve.
+4. **`implementation-plan.md`** — per-story specs (Q1-Q9 + Phase 0). SQL schemas, endpoint contracts, atomic claim CTE, failure-policy seed values, dependency watcher, needs_info TTL, knowledge service, self-healing heuristics, apprenticeship rules. Cutover sequence (30 min). Soak criteria.
+5. **`review.md`** — round-2 critique that produced Q7/Q8/Q9. Use this to see what the round-1 plan was missing.
+6. **`overwatch-runbook.md`** — ongoing operating model. Cadences, daily/weekly/monthly outputs, escalation matrix, cost-burn ceilings, freeze-break protocol, declining-overwatch ladder (Stage 0 → Stage 4).
+7. **`handoff.md`** — this file.
+
+## Open questions for your pass
+
+These are places I'm least confident; please attack:
+
+### Q-A. Is Q7's similarity threshold (0.75) right for question dedup?
+Too high → cache misses on legitimate matches; agents re-ask answered questions. Too low → false positives; agents get cached answers that don't apply. We have no calibration data because we've never run this. Suggest: ship at 0.75, instrument cache-hit precision via Morris weekly audit (sample 20 cache hits, label correct/incorrect), tune from there.
+
+### Q-B. Is the projection trigger (`dispatch_state_apply`) actually safe under high concurrency?
+The CTE in claim-next does a `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` on `dispatch_jobs`, INSERT into `dispatch_leases`, INSERT into `dispatch_v2_events` — all in one TX. The trigger then UPSERTs `dispatch_state_current`. Under high concurrency, two parallel claim-nexts could deadlock on the projection table. Need to verify: the trigger uses `ON CONFLICT (job_id) DO UPDATE` which should serialize cleanly, but the implementation-plan currently has `...` placeholders in the trigger function body. **Action item for next pass: write the actual trigger SQL, not pseudo-code.**
+
+### Q-C. The cross-repo dependency resolver uses `(repo, story_id)` as the dep key but `dispatch_jobs.story_id` is metadata, not unique. What if two repos both have STORY-740?
+Currently no story_id collision because we prefix with story-NNN globally, but this is a convention not a constraint. Either add `UNIQUE (repo, story_id)` to dispatch_jobs (trivial migration) or accept that dependency resolution may pick the wrong job. **Action item: decide and document.**
+
+### Q-D. Q9's pattern proposer clusters on `inputs_hash`. What inputs are normalized into the hash?
+The plan says "scope, failure_class, age, etc." but doesn't enumerate. If we hash too few fields → patterns generalize too aggressively (false rules); too many → no clusters ever form. **Action item: write down the canonical input set.** My initial proposal:
+- `decision_kind` (always)
+- `repo`, `scope`, `current_phase`
+- `failure_class` if applicable
+- `lane`
+- `age_bucket` (≤1h, 1-6h, 6-24h, 1-7d, >7d)
+NOT in the hash: `story_id`, `claimed_by`, exact timestamps, exact prompt text.
+
+### Q-E. The cutover assumes v1 and v2 endpoints can coexist. Have we proven this in staging?
+The implementation plan says we can mount v2 routes alongside v1 on the same FastAPI app. Routes are namespaced (`/api/dispatch/` vs. `/api/dispatch/v2/`), so technically yes. But the projection trigger fires on `dispatch_v2_events`, not `dispatch_events`, so v1 writes don't pollute v2 state. **Action item: dry-run the dual-mount on staging before committing to cutover sequence.**
+
+### Q-F. Does Q7's knowledge ingest actually work for failed stories?
+Plan says "after every successful story, extract decisions/anti-patterns." But we also want to extract from failed stories (anti-patterns are most valuable here). **Action item: extend Q7 to ingest from `failed` events too, classified separately as "learnings/anti-patterns" rather than "decisions/patterns."**
+
+### Q-G. The overwatch runbook's "Mark touch rate" metric counts decisions but not time. Is that the right axis?
+A 30-second decision counts the same as a 30-minute investigation. We probably want both: count AND elapsed time on each decision. Currently the dashboard would only have count. **Action item: should `dispatch_decisions` include `decision_duration_seconds`? Probably yes; cheap to capture.**
+
+### Q-H. We've made `dispatch_failure_policy` a mutable data table. What's the audit trail?
+If Mark changes a class's `max_attempts` from 3 to 1 at 14:30 and a story fails at 14:35, did it use the old or new policy? **Action item: add `dispatch_failure_policy_history` for change audit, OR enforce that policy changes always create new policy_version rows.**
+
+### Q-I. Apprenticeship counter-rules say "high-blast-radius decisions never auto-promote." Is the blocklist complete?
+Current list: cancel-in-flight, force-release, dead-letter, prune-cited-page, change-failure-class-ceiling. Probably missing: change-deploy-artifact-version, modify-policy-table, override-quarantine. **Action item: enumerate every `decision_kind`, mark each as auto-promotable or not, defend each non-auto in writing.**
+
+### Q-J. Do we have rollback for Q7's knowledge promotions?
+If Morris auto-promotes a wrong page and an agent reads it, the bad info is now in the wiki. Pruning helps but isn't real-time. **Action item: add `knowledge_promotions` audit log; allow Mark to one-click revert any auto-promote within 24h.**
+
+## What you can change without my approval
+
+- Tighten any AC that's vague.
+- Add tests I missed.
+- Renumber stories if you find a better sequence.
+- Replace pseudo-code with real SQL/Python.
+- Disagree with the freeze gate — but if you cut it, justify in writing.
+
+## What you should NOT change without my approval
+
+- The premise: "the queue's primitives are wrong" — if you think it's not architecture but pure policy churn, say so explicitly and propose the freeze-only path. Don't quietly skip Q1-Q6.
+- The decision gate (7-day freeze before Q1).
+- The declining-overwatch ratchet (Stage 0 → 4) — if you think this is overengineering, argue it; don't just remove it.
+- The non-goal list in `seed.md` — adding scope creep is the failure mode.
+
+## Pre-existing context to preserve
+
+This epic doesn't exist in isolation. Stories that have already shipped and represent learning:
+
+- **STORY-740** — dispatch state-machine contract test. v2's state machine in `architecture.md` § 4 is the canonical successor. The contract test pattern is reused for v2.
+- **STORY-700** — `dispatch_events` table exists. v2 uses `dispatch_v2_events` (separate, source of truth). Don't drop v1's events table; it's still our v1 audit trail.
+- **STORY-701** — failure classification taxonomy. Q3's policy table extends this; do not regress.
+- **STORY-702** — heartbeat + stale-claim release. Q1's lease table is the structural successor.
+- **STORY-707/727** — continuous self-improvement loop. Q9 is the structural successor.
+- **STORY-770/794** — `/answer-needs-info` skill. Q7's answer cache should integrate with this.
+- **STORY-773** — needs_info pattern detection (Morris Check 16). Q8's cluster-answer feature is the action layer on top of this detection.
+- **STORY-799** — OVERRIDE/DIRECTIVE.md injection above seed. Q7's `context-load` skill should respect and integrate with this; don't drop the directive layer.
+- **STORY-802** — Haiku/Sonnet migration. Cost ceilings in the runbook should align with the $20/day Foundry budget set there.
+
+## Final word
+
+You're being asked to do another pass on a plan that's already had two. Aim for one of three outcomes:
+
+1. **Approve as-is** with minor edits and a clear "ship it."
+2. **Approve with named changes** — list specifically what changes Q1-Q9 ACs or the runbook needs.
+3. **Reject** — argue that the entire premise is wrong (freeze-only, or different epic shape, or different decomposition).
+
+What I don't want is a third agent doing another half-pass that surfaces 20 new wrinkles we then have to evaluate. Be opinionated. Make a call.
