+
+---
+
+## Declining Overwatch — Mark's Cadence Must Shrink Quarterly
+
+**Principle:** every quarter, Mark's required touch rate goes down. If it doesn't, the system is regressing or Morris is not learning. This is the core test of "truly agentic" — not just running, but progressively self-running.
+
+### How it works (the apprenticeship loop)
+
+1. **Every operator decision is logged.** New table `dispatch_decisions(decision_id, decided_by, decided_at, decision_kind, inputs_hash, inputs_json, outcome, downstream_effect)`. Decisions captured: cancel, redispatch, force-release, answer-needs-info, approve PR, change priority, adjust failure-class policy, promote/reject knowledge page. Both Mark and Morris log here.
+
+2. **Pattern proposal.** Weekly job clusters Mark's decisions of each kind by `inputs_hash`. When ≥3 decisions in 90 days have similar inputs and the same outcome (e.g., "STORY in attention >7d with class=needs_info_unanswered → cancel"), Morris drafts a proposed rule and posts it in Mark's Friday Decision Note as `[CANDIDATE RULE]`.
+
+3. **Mark approves or refines.** A rule needs an explicit `RULE-APPROVED` tag from Mark to enter Morris's auto-policy. Default is "do not auto-apply." Approving is one click; ignoring keeps the manual step.
+
+4. **Approved rules become automation.** Morris executes the rule next time its trigger conditions match. Mark gets a daily summary line: `RULE-N fired X times, no overrides`.
+
+5. **Override tracking.** If Mark overrides a rule's outcome (e.g., resumes a story Morris auto-cancelled), the override is logged. Three overrides in 30 days → rule auto-disabled, Mark notified.
+
+6. **Soak-out.** After 90 days of zero overrides, the rule stops appearing in daily summaries. It's part of the system. Mark only hears about it if it ever fires badly.
+
+### Cadence reduction targets
+
+These targets are written down so we know if we're regressing. The default cadence (weekly Friday review, daily decision notes, monthly retro) is the **starting** position. Each quarter we should be moving down this ladder:
+
+| Stage | Mark's required cadence | Trigger to advance |
+|-------|------------------------|---------------------|
+| Stage 0 (start, 2026-Q2) | Daily decision note + weekly Friday review (~30 min) + monthly retro | — |
+| Stage 1 | Daily decision note (skim) + biweekly review + monthly retro | Mark touch rate <5/week for 4 weeks |
+| Stage 2 | Weekly summary only + monthly review | Mark touch rate <2/week for 8 weeks |
+| Stage 3 | Monthly summary + quarterly review | Mark touch rate <1/week for 12 weeks |
+| Stage 4 (target end-2026) | Quarterly review only; daily/weekly self-running | Mark touch rate <2/month for 12 weeks |
+
+**Mark touch rate** = count of `dispatch_decisions` rows where `decided_by='mark'` AND the decision was not a routine knowledge-layer promotion (those don't count). Counted in Morris's weekly trend.
+
+### Counter-rules (so the loop doesn't go off the rails)
+
+- **No rule auto-applies to a story type that's never been seen before.** A new repo or a new failure class always defaults to manual until ≥3 examples exist.
+- **High-blast-radius decisions never auto-promote.** Mark approval permanently required for: cancel an in-flight story, force-release a lease, dead-letter a job, prune a knowledge page that's been cited >10 times, change a failure-class ceiling.
+- **Override rate >10% kills automation.** If Mark overrides Morris's auto-decisions on >10% of cases in any 30-day window, all Morris auto-rules pause until Mark personally re-enables them. Drift detection.
+- **Quarterly review is mandatory and human.** The only mandatory Mark cadence that does not decline. 1 hour, every quarter, looks at touch rate trend, override rate, knowledge layer hit rate, what graduated and what got rolled back. Findings go in `tech-gc-knowledgebase/wiki/processes/overwatch-evolution.md`.
+
+### What "regression" looks like (so we can spot it)
+
+- Mark touch rate **going up** quarter over quarter.
+- Override rate **rising** for a previously stable rule (means the world changed, rule is now wrong).
+- Knowledge-layer hit rate **falling** (means agents are re-asking questions we already answered).
+- New incident classes appearing **faster than Morris adds rules** (means failure-class taxonomy is incomplete; back to the drawing board on Q3 policy).
+
+Any of these → quarterly review escalates to a full retro, and the next quarter's targets are revised down (more Mark involvement temporarily) until the trend reverses.
+
+### Anti-failure: when to *increase* Mark's involvement
+
+The ratchet is not one-way. Mark's involvement should go **up** if:
+- A net-new product line or repo enters the fleet (unfamiliar context, no prior rules apply).
+- A novel agent capability ships (e.g., new Claude model with different failure modes).
+- Override rate spikes for any reason.
+- The fleet expands (≥1 new agent) — old rules may not generalize.
+
+This is healthy and expected; the goal is decline over time on average, not monotonically.
+
+---
+
+## Failure modes of the runbook itself
+
+This runbook can fail in three predictable ways. Calling them out so future-Mark can spot the drift:
+
+1. **Ritual without signal.** Morris keeps posting briefs, Mark stops reading them. Mitigation: every brief must have at least one *actionable* line directed at Mark; if there's nothing, brief is omitted that day. Brief is a notification, not a journal.
+
+2. **Knowledge-layer rot.** Pages accumulate, citations don't. The base becomes archaeology. Mitigation: monthly "zero-citation prune" — any page never injected into a prompt in 90 days is automatically archived. Ruthless.
+
+3. **Escalation matrix mismatch.** Failure classes added in code, not in this matrix. Mitigation: CI gate — any new class added to `dispatch_failure_policy` must have a row in this table OR the build fails.
